d no\n105\ttiered delivery.\n106\t\n107\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n108\t\n109\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Coverage |\n110\t|-----|----------|------|----------|\n111\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Four audited flows fully keyboard-operable, no focus traps, no unreachable controls |\n112\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Consistent visible focus indicators across the audited flows |\n113\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | VPAT and accessibility statement updated and republished with technical review |\n114\t\n115\tFull coverage — no orphaned HLRs; every epic maps to at least one HLR.\n116\t\n117\t## Health Warnings\n118\t\n119\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n120\t   Teams table, Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, or\n121\t   NFRs. Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and the problem\n122\t   statement; owner teams were taken from architecture context (Dashboard) and\n123\t   inferred (Documentation).\n124\t2. **Documentation team inferred**: the Documentation component has no\n125\t   architecture-context file and the strategy names no owner. E002 is assigned\n126\t   to a documentation team by inference — verify before scheduling.\n127\t3. **Priority observation** (non-blocking): the problem statement ties the VPAT\n128\t   republication to two public-sector customer renewals, which arguably makes\n129\t   HLR-1/HLR-2 renewal-blocking (closer to P0 urgency) than their stated P1.\n130\t   Priorities are kept as stated in the strategy.\n131\t4. **Audit report as external input**: the audit report enumerating defects is\n132\t   referenced but not embedded in the strategy. E001 treats it as its defect\n133\t   list; if any enumerated defect turns out to require screen-reader semantics\n134\t   (explicitly out of scope), that item should be dispositioned out rather\n135\t   than silently dropped.\n136\t\n137\t## Tiered Delivery\n138\t\n139\tNot applicable — no external dep